5417] [17592186081075 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2421020d-932b-5f28-8f95-accb6c2498b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083132 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8869" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083132 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\textrm{LCM}(120, 90).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083132 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f524667d-8cd2-5b91-a9e0-538fc64399e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083132 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of two numbers, we first find the prime factorization of both numbers. \n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $120\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n![](images/fb/fb213f6ac09abbf31aacfdbfbd08b76dbea4199528abeb1c8041a73f0d7780ed.png)\n\nTherefore, $120 = 2^3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $90\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/03/034b3fe25edb2741abb035b8ca71a3c499fe6fae1a5975b4241d6379b819f19e.png)\n\nTherefore, $90 = 2 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 5.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120 & ={2}^{3}\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\ 90 & =2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $3$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common multiple of $120$ and $90$ is \n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(120, 90) = 2^3 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 5  = 360.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083133 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8870" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083133 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the lowest common denominator of $\\dfrac{4}{45}$ and $\\dfrac{7}{48}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083133 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c928766-9971-565a-b38b-5c97b2c3ee1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083133 #edb/ref 1074 "The lowest common denominator of two fractions is the least common multiple of the denominators.\n\nSo, to find the lowest common denominator of two fractions, we first find the prime factorization of both denominators.\n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $45\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/5e/5e53857c25c4b7fd8dd1897dcd1b0608e48512d5a61a200a11a3f8a0ca92fcd2.png)\n\nTherefore, $45= 3^2 \\cdot 5.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $48{:}$\n\n![](images/e2/e21d5cf36f2f08fbbb46e1d923d84e0b02f10531c42c590ab7d3a813f34009dc.png)\n\nTherefore, $48 = 2^4 \\cdot 3.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}45 & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 5 \\\\ 48 & ={2}^{4}\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $4$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common denominator is $2^4\\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 5 = 720.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083134 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8874" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083134 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\textrm{LCM}(30, 33, 20).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083134 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1373d2e3-fc83-56f2-b779-8f1e2d10efea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083134 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of three numbers, we first find their prime factorizations. \n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $30\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/31/312d8c33355fb36260cce172ab20bf907637528d0a5d04dc4dd1eceb59d765dd.png)\n\nTherefore, $30 = 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $33.$ We easily deduce that $33 = 3 \\cdot 11.$\n\n- Then, we find the prime factorization of $20{:}$\n\n![](images/7e/7e03a139fc3afa7df4d7f32ecf42f8f30d19bfe0415725f47fe5be63301899b5.png)\n\nTherefore, $20=2^2 \\cdot 5 .$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30 & =2\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\ 33 & =3\\cdot 11 \\\\ 20 & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $11$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest  common multiple of $30$, $33,$ and $20$ is \n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(30,  33, 20) = 2^2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 11  = 660.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083330 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083330 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083330 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083330 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083330 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083330 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083330 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Lowest Common Multiple of Two Integers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083330 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186083132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083330 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "384f8f04-972d-50cb-a023-dba29924242e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083331 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083331 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083331 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083331 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083331 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083331 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Lowest Common Denominator of Two Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083331 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186083133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083331 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c28dd57d-e6e5-5704-94a4-5f9bfb37f383" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083332 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083332 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083332