 prime factorization of both numbers. \n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $98:$ \n\n![](images/c7/c74979d4af6e34328dca1fab75fbf07c138c28ec8b93d5462d889f08ac6ea6c0.png)\n\nTherefore, $98=2\\cdot {7}^{2}.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $175:$\n\n![](images/56/56e885903dd09097a256b6740f7f6307ba2af3c0e43f32bb6456745acfb3e777.png)\n\n \nTherefore, $175=7\\cdot {5}^{2}.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}98 & =2\\cdot {7}^{2} \\\\ 175 & =7\\cdot {5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $7$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $2$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common multiple of $98$ and $175$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(98,175)=2\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{2}=2\\,450.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084277 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084277 #edb/ref 1061 33113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084277 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "853eea8e-7e8c-5972-b2df-6a996d469c6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084277 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084278 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084278 #edb/ref 1061 33116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084278 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0c8932d3-46c6-5cd6-941d-98887b4bfb0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084278 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084279 #edb/ref 1061 33118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084279 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "764cac2a-73a8-581d-8ccd-4d665b951e2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084279 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084280 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084280 #edb/ref 1061 33136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084280 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e705e91-32b9-5486-b6d0-c6ebfbc45c9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084280 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084281 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084281 #edb/ref 1061 34200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084281 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "44919f35-fd65-50af-b639-7d5eb83ab3b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084281 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084484 #edb/ref 1064 "The **lowest common multiple** (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of two integers is the smallest multiple that's common to both numbers.\n\nTo find the lowest common multiple of two small numbers, we can list all multiples of the two numbers and identify the smallest multiple that they have in common.\n\nFor example, let's find the lowest common multiple of $4$ and $6.$ We start by listing all multiples of $4$ and $6\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & \\,\\to \\,4,\\,8,\\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]12[/math]}},\\,16,\\,20,\\,24,\\,… \\\\ 6 & \\,\\to \\,6,\\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]12[/math]}},\\,18,\\,24,\\,30,\\,36,\\,…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe smallest multiple in both lists is ![](images/92/9285d37c7b455395acb42a54f78348bedfd8c9a72ad63d6e6ab7c2426148ea0d.svg) So $12$ is our answer, and we can write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(4,6)= 12.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The lowest common multiple is also sometimes called the *least* common multiple." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084484 #edb/ref 1065 5301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084484 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b16f2464-2c19-5166-bd61-97043695afcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084484 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084485 #edb/ref 1064 "Now suppose that we want to find the lowest common multiple of two larger numbers. It would take a while to make long lists of multiples. Luckily, there is a faster method. \n\nLet's demonstrate by finding $\\textrm{LCM}(10,12).$ We start by writing the prime factorization of $10$ and $12$, writing any repeating factors as powers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =2\\cdot 5={2}^{1}\\cdot {5}^{1} \\\\ 12 & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3={2}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write down each unique prime factor that appears in either of our decompositions. In our case, the unique prime factors are \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2}, \\qquad {\\color{red}3}, \\qquad {\\color{red}5}.\n$$\n\nNow, for each number in our list, we look at *both* prime factorizations and we take the *largest* power shown for each prime factor:\n\n- The factors corresponding to ${\\color{red}2}$ are ${\\color{red}2}^{\\color{blue}1}$ and ${\\color{red}2}^{\\color{blue}2}.$ Of these, the larger power is ${\\color{red}2}^{\\color{blue}2}.$\n\n- There is only one factor corresponding to ${\\color{red}3}.$ It is ${\\color{red}3}^{\\color{blue}1}.$\n\n- There is only one factor corresponding to ${\\color{red}5}.$ It is ${\\color{red}5}^{\\color{blue}1}.$\n\nFinally, we multiply the largest powers of each factor together, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(10,12) & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{1}\\cdot {5}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084485 #edb/ref 1065 5489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084485 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "de44c642-c9d2-5c1d-8b59-8781a99559ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084485 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Lowest Common Multiple of Two Larger Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493403 #edb/ref 1080 "19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493403 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493403 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0846cba1-214f-563e-8599-5c89551c8b0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493408 #edb/ref 1080 "26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493408 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493408 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a872d5e-744c-5b09-8f5b-a437fac96cbd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493414 #edb/ref 1080 "133" #edb/ref 17592186045417