61 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493883 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493883 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493883 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493883 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "edc669d6-a463-5d7f-9ce0-46adbba25422" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493890 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493890 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103757" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493890 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493890 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{LCM}(21,28,24).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493890 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02c3ac78-aa7f-5ad9-a0fe-7e15cb1f374c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493890 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of three numbers, we first find their prime factorizations. \n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $21.$ We easily deduce that $21=3\\cdot 7.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $28:$\n\n![](images/50/502509ed3e4392c8c4e3e8fa0b0e579c096f0b04108091f4d18a6f0e5a879e2a.png)\n\nTherefore, $28={2}^{2}\\cdot 7.$\n\n- Then, we find the prime factorization of $24:$\n\n![](images/cb/cbf192e5a1a1b1ac9db41bb9c8427ff8cdebeb697bcb618da87689a628b8f3af.png)\n\nTherefore, $24={2}^{3}\\cdot 3.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21 & =3\\cdot 7 \\\\ 28 & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 7 \\\\ 24 & ={2}^{3}\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $3$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $7$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest  common multiple of $21$, $28$  and $24$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(21,28,24)={2}^{3}\\cdot 3\\cdot 7=168.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493909 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493909 #edb/ref 1069 "q-7317" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493909 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493909 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the lowest common denominator of $\\frac{11}{30}$ and $\\frac{19}{64}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493909 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e11aa68-986c-58e0-bcdf-2852036febc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493909 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the lowest common denominator of two fractions, we first find the prime factorization of both denominators.\n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $30:$\n\n![](images/31/312d8c33355fb36260cce172ab20bf907637528d0a5d04dc4dd1eceb59d765dd.png)\n\nTherefore, $30=2\\cdot 3\\cdot 5.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $64:$\n\n![](images/44/447dca25b6cd3e19abd115869dac18c66b6066a37ef6181b4ac68e478aafb0b8.png)\n\nTherefore, $64={2}^{6}.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30 & =2\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\ 64 & ={2}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $6$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common denominator is ${2}^{6}\\cdot 3\\cdot 5=960.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493918 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493918 #edb/ref 1069 "q-171159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493918 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493918 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\text{LCM}(9,15,2)=${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493918 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42ddf56f-a8d0-56db-815a-a130a857fd56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493918 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of three numbers, we first find their prime factorizations. \n\n- First, we find the prime factorization of $9.$ We easily deduce that $9={3}^{2}.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $15.$ We easily deduce that $15=3\\times 5.$\n\n- Then, we notice that $2$ is a prime number.\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & ={3}^{2} \\\\ 15 & =3\\times 5 \\\\ 2 & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common multiple of $9$, $15,$ and $2$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(9,15,2)=2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 5=90.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493920 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493920 #edb/ref 1069 "q-7552" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493920 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493920 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{LCM}(7,19).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493920 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4852fb33-01b5-53c1-bda9-9f68b5cdfa3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493920 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of two numbers, we first find the prime factorization of both numbers. Since the numbers $7$ and $19$ are prime numbers, they are already each their respective prime factorizations. \n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $7$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $19$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common multiple of $7$ and $19$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(7,19)={7}^{1}\\cdot {19}^{1}=133.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493924 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493924 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43578" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493924 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493924 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{LCM}(14,18,63).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493924 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4dbbbcc8-62df-5e8e-bfa9-1313bcce5620" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493924 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of three numbers, we first find their prime factorizations. \n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $14.$ We easily deduce that $14=2\\cdot 7.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $18:$\n\n![](images/9e/9e7bddde8a287f6224b3e79f9fdacc3ac22d6db5a021b85df287e5e5fa321932.png)\n\nTherefore, $18=2\\cdot {3}^{2}.$\n\n- Then, we find the prime factorization of $63:$\n\n![](images/93/936a74a3795c2a8ace97dfddd7d4f2f359acf8968ea4404c2711abb96d6a708d.png)\n\nTherefore, $63={3}^{2}\\cdot 7.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =2\\cdot 7 \\\\ 18 & =2\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ 63 & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $7$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest  common multiple of $14$, $18$  and $63$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(14,18,63)=2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 7=126.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493959 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493959 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103752" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493959 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493959 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\text{LCM}(10,24,18)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493959 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca4901aa-e3e7-56b2-8843-d65effed5ee5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493959 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of three numbers, we first find their prime factorizations. \n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $10.$ We easily deduce that $10=2\\cdot 5.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $24:$\n\n![](images/cb/cbf192e5a1a1b1ac9db41bb9c8427ff8cdebeb697bcb618da87689a628b8f3af.png)\n\nTherefore, $24={2}^{3}\\cdot 3.$\n\n- Then, we find the prime factorization of $18:$\n\n![](images/9e/9e7bddde8a287f6224b3e79f9fdacc3ac22d6db5a021b85df287e5e5fa321932.png)\n\nTherefore, $18=2\\cdot {3}^{2}.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =2\\cdot 5 \\\\ 24 & ={2}^{3}\\cdot 3 \\\\ 18 & =2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $3$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest  common multiple of $10$, $24$  and $18$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(10,24,18)={2}^{3}\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 5=360.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493965 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493965 #edb/ref 1069 "q-7554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493965 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493965 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the lowest common denominator of $\\frac{2}{5}$ and $\\frac{8}{11}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493965 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dc2e2da4-0526-5cbb-b99c-ff4e18d482b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493965 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the lowest common denominator of two fractions, we first find the prime factorization of both denominators. \n\nSince the denominators $5$ and $11$ are prime numbers, they are already represented by their respective prime factorizations. \n\nNow, we write down each prime factor, and we raise each factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $11$ is also $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common denominator is ${5}^{1}\\cdot {11}^{1}=55.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
